Checking the PDF the MuPDF renderer writes.

    src/Plugins/MuPDF/tests/pdf-compare.sh [document.tm ...]

Every TeXmacs it starts has a home of its own (a copy of ~/.TeXmacs, copy
on write), so that nothing of the user's is changed and the runs side by
side share nothing; JOBS (4) of them run at a time, first the exports and
then the checks, which takes some 40 s for the whole set. REF=1 exports
every document through the other route of the build as well, for its
size; EMBED=0 leaves out the round trip of an embedded document
(embed-roundtrip.sh: exported with its source, found by mutool extract,
imported with the source folder gone, and a file next to the PDF left
alone); encrypt-check.sh exports with passwords and restricted
permissions and checks that no reader opens the PDF without a password,
that both passwords do, that it is AES-256 and that only what was asked
is allowed. pdf-check.js adds MuPDF's own check of the structure
(PDFDocument.check, MuPDF 1.27 and later) to every document; it looks at
the cross reference table and the syntax of the objects, not at what the
objects mean -- the outline which pointed at a link annotation passed it,
which is what structure-check.py and dest-bytes.py are for.



from the top of the source tree; with no argument it takes the documents
listed in the script, some of the manual and the ones here. It exports each
one through the MuPDF renderer (TEXMACS_PDF_MUPDF=1) and checks it:

  gs      Ghostscript reads the file without an error and without
          substituting a font. This is the check which earns its keep: an
          invalid font program is the mistake which is easiest to make and
          hardest to see, since the page still looks like a page, only in
          the wrong typeface.
  text    the text extracts, and no character comes out as U+FFFD (a wrong
          encoding, a missing glyph name, a stale ToUnicode).
  lig     every ligature glyph says which letters it stands for
          (/ActualText), or a search for "first" finds nothing in a reader
          which takes the text from the glyph names. Neither reader judges
          this alone: MuPDF decomposes ligatures whatever the file says,
          and Ghostscript 10 ignores ActualText. That second blindness is
          what makes the check -- Ghostscript's extraction counts the
          ligature glyphs drawn, and the content streams must hold a span
          for each. Removing the spans makes it fail; that was tried.
  render  the page Ghostscript draws and the page MuPDF draws agree to
          within a threshold, once both are blurred by a pixel and a half
          (the readers anti-alias and resample differently; measured, the
          documents agree at 97.5% and more, a real fault at 42%). They are two independent readers of the same
          file, so a disagreement is a fault in the file -- but note that
          it does *not* catch a substituted font, since both readers then
          substitute; that is what the gs check is for.
  size    reported, not judged. The two routes do not embed the same kind
          of font -- the other one goes through PostScript and comes back
          with bitmaps -- so the numbers are not comparable.

The checks were themselves checked: embedding the Type 1 fonts the wrong
way again (pdf_add_cid_font instead of pdf_add_simple_font) makes the gs
and text checks fail, as it should.

Some of the documents are here rather than in the manual: figures.tm has
a PostScript figure and a raster one, which must come out as a /Form and
an /Image and not as pictures of themselves; pdf-figures.tm has two PDF
figures, vector.pdf and the same turned by /Rotate 90, whose text says
which way is up -- the one check which is not generic: the harness reads
where UP lands against the caption, since the rendering check cannot see
a figure upside down (both readers draw the same file); taking the fix out
makes it fail, that was tried. It also has overlap.pdf, two overlapping
squares, at half opacity: as a transparency group the overlap is pale
blue, without one purple (the blue square over a half transparent red
one), and the harness counts purple pixels in what each reader draws --
289 and 324 without the group, 0 with it; pattern.tm has a patterned
background of opaque tiles, which must be one /Pattern; pattern-photo.tm
has a photograph with a soft mask as its tile, which must *not* be one,
since Ghostscript draws such a pattern wrongly (and the check catches it
when it is); ligatures.tm is a line of fi, ffi, ffl and fl in the EC font;
landscape.tm must come out wider than it is tall; layer.tm includes
layer.pdf (made by mklayer.js), a figure with a layer which is off by
default, which must stay hidden in the PDF; structure.tm has a table
of contents three levels deep, labels and references, one on a second page,
a link to a web page and a title, and was saved by TeXmacs, so that it
carries the values of its labels (a forward reference is "?" otherwise).
structure.js (mutool run) with structure-check.py checks that the outline,
the named destinations, the links -- the web one included, which a batch
export used to lose -- and the title are there, that every entry of the
outline points at its parent by reference, and that MuPDF has nothing to
repair: the parts the writer assembles when the document is closed. It has
accents in a heading, a label, the author and an address, and dest-bytes.py
checks the file itself: every link names its destination in the bytes of a
key of the name tree (a reader compares bytes), and every URI is ASCII.

Needs gs, mutool, and python3 with PIL. OUT, WAIT and AGREE override the
output directory, how long a conversion is given, and the threshold.
